+    {
+      "key": "gaussian-surrogate-obstruction",
+      "namespace": "probability.core",
+      "logical_name": "p0321-cutoff-gaussian-elastic-surrogate-correlations",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Cutoff Gaussian elastic surrogate has algebraic positional correlation and an orientation plateau",
+      "statement": "For a centered real 2D Gaussian displacement field with stationary increments and cutoff spectrum Q(k)=a_T|k|^{-2}P_T(k)+a_L|k|^{-s}P_L(k) on 0<|k|<=Lambda, where a_T>0, a_L>=0, 0<=s<2, the density-wave surrogate E exp(i q·(u(re)-u(0))) lies between positive multiples of r^{-a_T|q|^2/(4pi)} for every fixed nonzero q and direction e. For theta=curl(u)/2, the sixfold surrogate E exp(i6(theta(re)-theta(0))) tends to exp(-36 a_T Lambda^2/(16pi))>0. These are surrogate-field facts, not Riesz Gibbs or Delaunay-bond statements.",
+      "proof_text": "Gaussianity gives E exp(iq·Delta u)=exp[-Var(q·Delta u)/2]. Polar integration yields transverse variance a_T|q|^2/(2pi) times integral_0^{Lambda r}[1-J0(t)-cos(2alpha)J2(t)]dt/t = a_T|q|^2 log r/(2pi)+O(1), since Bessel tails divided by t are integrable; the longitudinal term is O(1) for s<2. The curl spectrum is a_T/4 on the cutoff disk, so Var theta=a_T Lambda^2/(16pi), and its covariance tends to zero by Riemann-Lebesgue. The Gaussian characteristic function then gives the stated orientation limit.",
+      "assumptions": ["Only the stipulated Gaussian field, not a particle Gibbs process", "Fixed ultraviolet cutoff Lambda>0", "q is nonzero and e is a fixed unit direction"],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {"source_id": "owner:w541:p0321-gaussian-20261010", "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w541/INDEPENDENT.md", "revision": "reviewed-by-w542-2026-10-10", "license": "owner-own-work", "excerpt": null}
+    },
+    {
+      "key": "cumulant-vertex-bound",
+      "namespace": "probability.core",
+      "logical_name": "bounded-cumulants-imply-algebraic-vertex-bounds",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Uniform higher cumulants and logarithmic variance imply algebraic vertex bounds",
+      "statement": "Let q,a>0 and D,B>=0. For each real X_{L,r}, 2<=r<=L/4, assume E exp(q|X_{L,r}|)<infinity, |kappa_2(X_{L,r})-a log r|<=D, and sum_{n>=3}q^n|kappa_n(X_{L,r})|/n!<=B uniformly. Set eta=q^2 a/2 and H=q^2D/2+B. Then exp(-H)r^{-eta}<=|E exp(iqX_{L,r})|<=exp(H)r^{-eta}. This conditional lemma does not establish the hypotheses for Riesz gases.",
+      "proof_text": "The moment-generating function M(z)=E exp(zX) is analytic for |Re z|<q. The absolutely convergent cumulant series K(z)=sum_n kappa_n z^n/n! is analytic in |z|<q and continuous at iq. Near zero M=exp K, hence throughout the disk by the identity theorem. Passing z=itq to iq by dominated and absolute convergence gives M(iq)=exp K(iq). Its real exponent is -q^2 kappa_2/2 plus a remainder bounded by B, yielding the bounds.",
+      "assumptions": ["Uniform exponential moment at q", "Uniform log-variance estimate", "Absolute sum of higher cumulants at q"],
